        <section class="number-world-receipt" id="number-world-geometry-receipt" aria-labelledby="number-world-receipt-title">
          <header class="number-world-receipt__header">
            <div>
              <p class="study-kicker">Number World · geometry receipt</p>
              <h3 id="number-world-receipt-title">The mesh can be read without turning its addresses into identities.</h3>
            </div>
            <p>The receipt fixes three different layers: exact integer relations, the Euler mesh ledger and one declared decimal readout. The readout is reproducible for these source meshes, but it is resolution-dependent and therefore not a new topological invariant.</p>
          </header>

          <div class="number-world-receipt__operators" aria-label="Number World operators and exact equations">
            <article>
              <span>01 · CORE / CLAMP / MOTION</span>
              <strong><i>b</i> = 8</strong>
              <code>K(b)=2b+1=17</code>
              <code>M(b)=3b+1=25</code>
              <p><b>17 = 8|1|8</b> is the declared clamp. <b>25 = 3·8+1</b> is the three-block-plus-witness expansion.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <span>02 · EULER CLAMP</span>
              <strong>χ = V − E + F</strong>
              <code>E=V+F−χ</code>
              <p>The sign word is <b>(+V, −E, +F)</b>. Edge count <i>E</i> is the connection term between vertices and faces, not a free Number-Game substitution.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <span>03 · WHITE 19 AXIS</span>
              <strong>A<sub>FV</sub>(M)=⌊10F/V⌋=19</strong>
              <code>D(M)=(A<sub>FV</sub>, ⌊100·&#123;10F/V&#125;⌋)</code>
              <p>The first readout digit pair remains <b>19</b> across the listed triangulated carriers. The residual supplies a second, explicitly quantized address.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <span>04 · TWO PRIME WITNESSES</span>
              <strong>D(Splinter) = 19|61</strong>
              <code>10·355/181 = 19.613259…</code>
              <strong>D(Boundary) = 19|83</strong>
              <code>10·119/60 = 19.833333…</code>
              <p><b>61</b> and <b>83</b> are prime, but primality is a property of the resulting addresses—not the cause of the mesh ratios.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <span>05 · 96-SHELL / RED WITNESS</span>
              <strong>96 = 12·8 = 6·16 = 3·32</strong>
              <code>63=64−1=2<sup>6</sup>−1</code>
              <p>The Red Balloon address <b>63</b> and prime witnesses <b>61, 83</b> all lie inside the declared finite address set <b>V<sub>96</sub>=&#123;1,…,96&#125;</b>.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <span>06 · NUMBER-WORLD ENVELOPE</span>
              <strong>1001 = 10<sup>3</sup>+1 = 7·11·13</strong>
              <code>V<sub>96</sub> ⊂ N<sub>1001</sub>=&#123;0,…,1001&#125;</code>
              <code>X<sub>10</sub>(n)=n+10</code>
              <p>The ×10 stick moves along a decimal column while preserving the final digit: <b>19→29→39→…→99</b>. Integer <b>1001</b> remains distinct from decimal <b>1.001</b>.</p>
            </article>
          </div>

